/// The dashboard theme CONTRACT — just the [[Theme]] class. Implementations
/// live in sibling modules, one per look:
///
///   - `theme-beer.pkl` — BeerCSS Material Design 3, the DEFAULT (wired in
///     `entry.pkl`) and currently the only shipped implementation.
///
/// A new theme is a sibling module exporting a `theme: Theme` instance; an
/// entry overrides its look by assigning `theme`, e.g.
/// `theme = (import "lib/theme-mine.pkl").theme`, and can retune any theme by
/// amending the instance (`tokens`/`styles` are ordinary properties; see the
/// implementation modules for their amend forms).
///
/// Everything visual lives outside the server. The renderer emits `tokens` as
/// `:root` custom properties (with `tokensDark` under prefers-color-scheme:dark);
/// the server `<link>`s `stylesheets`, injects `scripts` as ES modules, and
/// inlines `styles` + `inlineScripts`.
///
/// ## A theme is the THIRD layer, not the only one (ADR 0020)
///
/// The page's CSS is assembled from three sources, in this order, all inside one
/// `<style id="fh-theme">`:
///
///   1. `core/css.pkl`'s `baseCss` — the `fh-` layout contract, the `--fh-*`
///      colour variables, and the classes the RUNTIME emits or binds
///      (`fh-disabled`/`fh-loading`, the offline banners, the toast). `entry.pkl`
///      puts it on the dashboard itself, so it is not yours to drop.
///   2. every card's `cardDef.css` — the structure of the markup that card
///      emits, written against the `--fh-*` variables.
///   3. this theme's `styles`.
///
/// Later beats earlier by document order, so `styles` can override anything in
/// 1 or 2 without either of them knowing. What is genuinely YOURS is the paint:
/// the palette, the `--fh-*` re-pointing, your framework's own class names, and
/// any retune of the two layers below.
///
/// ## What a card guarantees you
///
/// The class contract cards and the renderer emit, which your `styles` may
/// restyle (all of it already has a working default from layers 1-2):
///   containers -> `.fh-grid` / `.fh-row` / `.fh-col`, the universal node
///   wrapper -> `.fh-cell`, a query set's member container -> `.fh-group`
///   (ALWAYS a pure CSS passthrough, not an author-facing choice), per-cell
///   layout classes -> `.fh-cols-<1..12>`/`.fh-cols-full`, a grid's
///   group-centering opt-out -> `.fh-start`, cards -> `.card`, section titles ->
///   `.section`, state text -> `.state`, popup dialog -> `.popup`/`.popup-close`,
///   tabs -> `.tabs`/`.tabbar`/`.tab`, more-info facts ->
///   `.entity-info`/`.entity-info-id`/`.entity-info-attrs`, the slider's parts ->
///   `.slider-card`/`.slider-head`/`.slider-icon`/`.slider-sub`/`.slider-action`/
///   `.slider-body`/`.slider-members`/`.slider-group`.
///
/// A guarded action in flight toggles two classes on the tappable element
/// itself: `.fh-disabled` (the guard is active, the element is genuinely inert)
/// and `.fh-loading` (the "still working" cursor). Both are IMMEDIATE, and a
/// theme must keep them so: this is the answer to the tap.
///
/// ## Getting your OWN class names into a card's markup
///
/// The SPINNER is separate and DELAYED, because "this is taking a while" is a
/// different statement from "your tap landed". It is also the one place where
/// the right class depends on the framework, so [[classes]] is how you name it:
/// the card templates carry a hole (`core/node.pkl`'s `CLASS_BIND`) that the
/// card registry fills with the names you list. BeerCSS asks for
/// `shape loading-indicator` — its own self-morphing SVG mask — and a theme that
/// asks for nothing gets `fh-busy-spin`, the plain ring in `core/css.pkl`.
/// Style whichever you name as "showing", with no delay of its own: the
/// decision was already made before the class landed (the delay is a derived
/// signal in `tap.pkl`). Bind rather than style-inert — BeerCSS's
/// `.shape>i{filter:invert(1)}` is unconditional and would tint the glyph at
/// rest.
///
/// A component opts out of the whole busy look via `busyVisual = false` (it then
/// binds none of it); the guard stays. A failed action shows `.fh-toast`, a small
/// fixed bar created by the shell on `datastar-fetch:error` — structure and
/// behavior live in the shell, the LOOK is layer 1's, and yours to retune.
///
/// ## WRITING A THEME (the whole API surface)
///   1. Create a sibling `theme-<name>.pkl` exporting `theme: Theme` (or an
///      `open class` extending [[Theme]] if you want amendable knobs, like
///      theme-beer's palettes).
///   2. `tokens`/`tokensDark`: HA-named design tokens (see tokens.pkl) —
///      emitted as `:root` custom properties, dark under
///      prefers-color-scheme. `core/css.pkl` defaults every `--fh-*` variable
///      the cards read from these, so a theme that sets only tokens already
///      renders coherently.
///   3. `stylesheets`/`scripts`: your framework's CDN links (pinned versions,
///      never `@latest`). Scripts are ES modules injected after stylesheets;
///      dashboard BEHAVIOR stays with Datastar — ship JS only for visuals
///      your CSS needs (e.g. BeerCSS's slider track fill), and verify its
///      bindings survive SSE morphs (delegated/document-level listeners do).
///      `inlineScripts` is the same rule for JS you write yourself rather than
///      link — a pointer gesture your rules depend on, like
///      [[sliderHoldScript]]. Both force a page RELOAD when they change (a
///      script cannot be un-run), unlike `styles`, which patches.
///   4. `styles`: the palette, then the `--fh-*` re-pointing if your framework
///      has better roles than the HA tokens, then whatever your framework's own
///      classes need. You do NOT need to restate the layout contract or any
///      card's structure — they arrive ahead of you.
///   5. `classes`: the class names you want injected into card markup (today:
///      `busySpin`).
///   6. `chrome`: the page frame — MUST wrap `{{{body}}}` in an element with
///      `id="dashboard"` (the reload swap target; enforced by
///      Dashboard.validate) and, if the dashboard uses popups, host a
///      `<div id="popups">{{{popups}}}</div>` overlay mount. The hole is where
///      a popup being RESTORED on a refresh is baked, so it is on screen with
///      the first paint instead of arriving when the stream connects; leave it
///      out and the dialog still works, it just pops in a moment late.
///   7. An entry opts in with `theme = (import "lib/theme-<name>.pkl").theme`.
module theme

import "core/node.pkl" as nodes

/// Axis-intent gate before a TOUCH may move a slider, so a dashboard of slider
/// cards is still a scrollable list. Without it every swipe that happens to
/// start on a card sets a brightness, and a tap meant to stop a fling sets one
/// too. Pair it with the CSS half — on coarse pointers the range input must be
/// `pointer-events:none` and its wrapper `touch-action:pan-y` (that half lives
/// with the slider card, in `components/slider.pkl`); `.fh-armed` lands on the
/// wrapper once armed, for whatever affordance the theme wants.
///
/// Tracking starts on `pointerdown`, but the gesture isn't armed yet: each
/// `pointermove` is checked against the along-axis (`x`) and cross-axis (`y`)
/// deltas from the start point. Cross-axis movement past `C` that dominates
/// the along-axis delta reads as a scroll — `off()`, and the still-unclaimed
/// pointer falls through to the browser's native `touch-action:pan-y`.
/// Along-axis movement past `S` arms the drag. A stationary tap (the
/// fling-stop case above) never crosses either threshold, so it is a no-op by
/// construction — no timer needed for that either. This replaces an earlier
/// fixed-delay (press-and-hold) gate: the delay was uniform jank on every
/// touch, armed or not, where a threshold only slows down the gestures that
/// were genuinely ambiguous.
///
/// The value is driven from here rather than handed to the range input once
/// armed, because it cannot be handed over: a synthesized `pointerdown` is
/// untrusted, and untrusted events do not start a native drag. So this maps
/// x -> value itself, dispatching `input` (which repaints the fill and updates
/// the bound signal) on every move once armed, and `change` (the card's
/// `data-on:change` POST) once, on release. Mouse gestures are left entirely
/// alone.
///
/// Keyed on `.slider.max`, which the slider CARD emits (components/slider.pkl),
/// so it is reusable by any theme that keeps that markup.
const sliderHoldScript: String = """
  (()=>{const S=6,C=10;
  let w=null,i=null,x0=0,y0=0,pid=0,armed=false;
  const off=()=>{if(w){w.classList.remove('fh-armed');try{w.releasePointerCapture(pid)}catch(e){}}
  w=null;i=null;armed=false};
  const put=(cx,commit)=>{if(!w||!i)return;const r=w.getBoundingClientRect();if(!r.width)return;
  const mn=+i.min||0,mx=+i.max||100,st=+i.step||1,
  p=Math.min(1,Math.max(0,(cx-r.left)/r.width)),
  v=Math.min(mx,Math.max(mn,Math.round((mn+p*(mx-mn))/st)*st));
  if(v!=+i.value){i.value=v;i.dispatchEvent(new Event('input',{bubbles:true}))}
  if(commit)i.dispatchEvent(new Event('change',{bubbles:true}))};
  document.addEventListener('pointerdown',e=>{if(e.pointerType==='mouse')return;
  const el=e.target&&e.target.closest&&e.target.closest('.slider.max');if(!el)return;
  off();const inp=el.querySelector('input[type=range]');if(!inp)return;
  w=el;i=inp;x0=e.clientX;y0=e.clientY;pid=e.pointerId},true);
  document.addEventListener('pointermove',e=>{if(!w)return;
  const dx=e.clientX-x0,dy=e.clientY-y0;
  if(!armed){
  if(Math.abs(dy)>C&&Math.abs(dy)>Math.abs(dx)){off();return}
  if(Math.abs(dx)<S)return;
  armed=true;w.classList.add('fh-armed');
  try{w.setPointerCapture(pid)}catch(e){}
  if(navigator.vibrate)navigator.vibrate(8)}
  put(e.clientX,false)},true);
  const end=e=>{if(!w)return;if(armed&&e.type==='pointerup')put(e.clientX,true);off()};
  document.addEventListener('pointerup',end,true);
  document.addEventListener('pointercancel',end,true)})();
  """

open class Theme {
  tokens: Mapping<String, String>
  tokensDark: Mapping<String, String>
  stylesheets: Listing<String>
  /// External JS URLs, `<script type="module" src>`-injected in the document
  /// head (after the stylesheets, before Datastar). ES modules — deferred, so
  /// they run after the first paint. For framework helpers a theme's CSS needs
  /// (e.g. BeerCSS's slider fill); dashboard behavior stays Datastar's.
  scripts: Listing<String> = new {}
  /// Classic `<script>` bodies inlined in the head, run BEFORE first paint —
  /// the gesture half of a CSS interaction, for what CSS alone cannot express
  /// (see [[sliderHoldScript]]). Dashboard BEHAVIOR still stays Datastar's:
  /// this is for a pointer gesture the theme's own rules depend on, not for
  /// talking to the server.
  inlineScripts: Listing<String> = new {}
  styles: String
  chrome: String
  /// Class names this theme wants injected into card markup, `key -> one or
  /// more class names` (space-separated). Today's only key is `busySpin` — the
  /// delayed spinner (see the module doc). An unlisted key binds nothing.
  ///
  /// `hidden`: it is consumed while the card registry is built
  /// (`core/node.pkl`'s `cardsWith`, called from `entry.pkl`), so it never
  /// reaches the wire and the runtime never learns a theme had an opinion.
  hidden classes: Mapping<String, String> = nodeDefaults
}

// The fallback names, so a theme amending `classes` starts from the working
// default rather than from nothing. A module-level `const` because a class body
// may only reference those.
const local nodeDefaults: Mapping<String, String> = nodes.defaultClasses
